      "text": "Let's start with the given functional equation:\n\nFor all non-zero \\( x \\), we have:\n\\[ f(x) + 2f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 3x. \\tag{1} \\]\n\nWe are asked to find the real solutions to \\( f(x) = f(-x) \\). First, let's substitute \\( x \\) with \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\) in Equation (1):\n\n\\[ f\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) + 2f(x) = \\frac{3}{x}. \\tag{2} \\]\n\nNow we have two equations:\n1. \\( f(x) + 2f\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 3x. \\tag{1} \\)\n2. \\( f\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) + 2f(x) = \\frac{3}{x}. \\tag{2} \\)\n\nLet's solve these equations simultaneously. Multiply Equation (1) by 2:\n\\[ 2f(x) + 4f\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 6x. \\tag{3} \\]\n\nNow we have:\n\\[ 2f(x) + 4f\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 6x, \\]\n\\[ f\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) + 2f(x) = \\frac{3}{x}. \\tag{3'} \\]\n\nExpress \\( f\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\) from Equation (2):\n\\[ f\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\frac{3}{x} - 2f(x). \\tag{4} \\]\n\nNow substitute Equation (4) into Equation (3):\n\\[ 2f(x) + 4\\left( \\frac{3}{x} - 2f(x) \\right) = 6x. \\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[ 2f(x) + \\frac{12}{x} - 8f(x) = 6x, \\]\n\\[ -6f(x) + \\frac{12}{x} = 6x. \\]\n\nCombine like terms to form a quadratic equation:\n\\[ -6f(x) = 6x - \\frac{12}{x}, \\]\n\\[ 6f(x) = \\frac{12}{x} - 6x, \\]\n\\[ 6f(x) = -6x + \\frac{12}{x}, \\]\n\\[ f(x) = -x + \\frac{2}{x}. \\]\n\nNow we want to solve \\( f(x) = f(-x) \\):\n\\[ -x + \\frac{2}{x} = -(-x) + \\frac{2}{-x}, \\]\n\\[ -x + \\frac{2}{x} = x - \\frac{2}{x}. \\]\n\nCombine like terms to form another quadratic equation:\n\\[ -x + \\frac{2}{x} = x - \\frac{2}{x}, \\]\n\\[ -x - x = -\\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{2}{x}, \\]\n\\[ -2x = -\\frac{4}{x}, \\]\n\\[ -2x^2 = -\\frac{4}{x}, \\]\n\\[ 2x^3 = 4, \\]\n\\[ x^3 = 2. \\]\n\nTherefore, the real solution to \\( f(x) = f(-x) \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt[3]{2}}\n\\]",
